ess assumptions. Together they support a topic centered on distinguishing demonstrable sampling advantage from broadly useful algorithmic advantage.\",\n      \"heading\": \"Task-specific demonstrations versus reusable advantage\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"body\": \"The available evidence supports a cautious synthesis: advantage is credible for selected tasks and models, but each claimed gain depends on hardware errors, data loading, readout, circuit costs, and comparison against optimized classical methods.\",\n      \"heading\": \"Evidence boundaries\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"source_ids\": [\n    \"source-05-quantum-machine-learning--a91f0184eb29\",\n    \"source-01-quantum-computing-in-the-nisq-era-and-beyond--2ce59b8ba8bb\",\n    \"source-02-quantum-supremacy-using-a-programmable-superconducting-processor--f98990eb22d8\"\n  ],\n  \"structure_type\": \"wiki\",\n  \"title\": \"Practical quantum advantage depends on benchmarks, access models, and classical baselines\",\n  \"topic_id\": \"topic-practical-quantum-advantage-benchmarks-and-bottlenecks\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
